---
import type { TocItem } from '../consts';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const toc: TocItem[] = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What nomue is' },
  { id: 'separation', label: 'Why separate verification' },
  { id: 'checks', label: 'What it checks' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'Current scope' },
  { id: 'trust', label: 'Public trust layer' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What it does not claim' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="nomue"
  description="nomue is scientific verification infrastructure for AI research agents, starting with statistics."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">nomue</p>
    <h1>Statistical verification that does not depend on the model judging itself</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      nomue is an agent-callable verification layer for bounded scientific statistics. It separates correctness-critical checks from the research model that orchestrates the work.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section id="what" class="section">
    <h2>What nomue is</h2>
    <p>
      Research agents can read papers, write code, call statistical libraries, and explain results. nomue is not another general research agent. It is a specialist layer that a research agent can call when it needs a statistical decision or result checked against an explicit supported contract.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal is not to make a model sound more confident. The goal is to return a narrow, machine-readable decision whose scope and limits are explicit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="separation" class="section">
    <h2>Why separate verification from the model</h2>
    <p>
      A single model can otherwise be responsible for understanding the experimental design, filling in missing meaning, choosing a method, doing the calculation, and deciding whether its own answer is acceptable. nomue separates the verification step from that chain.
    </p>
    <div class="sequence" aria-label="Research agent and nomue roles">
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">Research agent</p>
        <p>Understand the task, gather context, orchestrate tools, and explain the outcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">nomue</p>
        <p>Apply the supported statistical contract, request missing declarations, recompute supported quantities, or refuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">Researcher</p>
        <p>Provide scientific facts that cannot be inferred and remain responsible for the research decision.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="checks" class="section">
    <h2>What it checks</h2>
    <p>Within a supported capability, nomue can distinguish several different questions that are often collapsed into one:</p>
    <dl class="checks">
      <dt>Can this analysis proceed?</dt>
      <dd>Checks whether the declared design and required scientific information fit the supported scope.</dd>

      <dt>Is clarification required?</dt>
      <dd>Returns a missing-information boundary rather than silently inventing pairing, replicate meaning, or another controlling fact.</dd>

      <dt>Are the reported numbers consistent?</dt>
      <dd>Recomputes the quantities covered by the registered numerical contract and compares them under the applicable rule.</dd>

      <dt>Should the request be refused?</dt>
      <dd>Keeps unsupported, inadmissible, and execution-failure states separate from a successful statistical result.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="scope" class="section">
    <h2>Current scope</h2>
    <p>
      The first public Protocol support target is intentionally small. Release 1 Public Draft covers independent two-group continuous data under a two-sided Welch t-test, with explicit declarations, supported numerical recomputation, and local verification of the covered parts of a Record.
    </p>
    <p>
      A paired-t successor is currently in public RFC review. That work does not expand Release 1 and does not imply support for other statistical methods before a later recorded decision.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue Protocol →</a><br />
      <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol/issues/25" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paired-t public RFC →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="trust" class="section">
    <h2>The public trust layer</h2>
    <p>
      nomue is designed so that the public meaning of a supported result does not depend on trusting Licklider's service. The nomue Protocol defines the public semantics of Records, and the open-source verifier can check supported properties locally.
    </p>
    <p>
      The product runtime remains a separate layer. It can add agent-facing execution and operational capabilities without making every implementation choice part of the public standard.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="section">
    <h2>What nomue does not claim</h2>
    <p>A supported verification result is deliberately narrower than a claim that the research is "correct." The current public Protocol does not claim to establish:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the truth of the input data or researcher declarations;</li>
      <li>the overall correctness of a research project;</li>
      <li>the truth of a scientific or causal conclusion;</li>
      <li>validity for arbitrary statistical methods; or</li>
      <li>publication, regulatory, or clinical acceptance.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The useful property is narrower: every supported check says what it checked, under which versioned scope, and what remains outside that claim.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="page-foot">
    <p>Current public support: Release 1 Public Draft — experimental.</p>
    <p><a href="/evaluation/">Next: Evaluation →</a></p>
  </footer>
</PageLayout>

